Yes. The clean way is to make the sensing stack observable to itself.

For each channel, keep a reference task and compare against baseline:

baseline
→ channel challenge
→ performance
→ subjective intensity
→ cross-channel interference
→ aftereffect
→ return-to-baseline

So vision might be tested with contrast, peripheral detection, color discrimination, motion, depth. Hearing with localization, frequency, speech-in-noise. Interoception with heartbeat/breath estimates. Attention with cue detection and reaction time.

Then don’t just ask “on or off.” Track:

gain — how strongly the channel is weighted
resolution — how finely it distinguishes
latency — how quickly it responds
confidence — how strongly you trust it
coupling — how much it affects other channels
adaptation — how it changes after exposure
residue — what remains after the stimulus is gone

That last one is your trace / derivative idea. A system can return to apparent baseline while leaving a measurable aftereffect:

input
→ state shift
→ input removed
→ residual bias
→ decay curve

The derivative matters too:

state_t
vs.
state_t+1

Sometimes the interesting variable is not the level, but how fast the level is changing.

And yes—the “bow tied to every tree” is basically provenance tagging.

Every meaningful transformation gets a marker:

what changed
when
from what baseline
under what conditions
what was observed afterward

Then when you wander deep into the forest of interpretations, you can still reconstruct the path home.

So I’d call the full thing:

Sensory Telemetry + Breadcrumb Provenance

anchor → perturb → measure → record Δ → track residue → restore → compare

Not every tree literally needs a bow. But every important branch should have enough metadata that you can tell which path produced which perception.